      "text": "Let $\\mathcal{F}$ be the set of positive divisors of the number $m$ that remains in the list after Carolyn's turn.\n\nWe first check that the number $m$ must be a prime. Suppose there exists a divisor $k$ in $\\mathcal{F}$ but there exists no numbers $n$ such that $\\gcd(k, n) \\neq \\gcd(k, n^2)$. Since $k$ divides both $n$ and $n^2$, this forces $k$ divides them evenly or not at all. However, we cannot put $n$ on the list since we have already removed all larger divisors of it or none at all, forcing $k$ to be divisible by $n$. We repeat this process until $n$ reaches $1$, meaning all numbers have been removed, contradicting the rule. Thus, a prime $m$ exists.\n\nKnowing that $m$ is prime, we apply the following strategy: Carolyn starts in favor of Paul by removing $m$ on her first turn. Paul then must remove all other numbers but $1$, since $1$ divides $m$ evenly but not $m^2$. That is, $\\mathcal{F}$ only contains $m$ itself. Therefore, Carolyn cannot remove any other number. Paul removes all numbers, and thus the sum of Carolyn's numbers removed is $m + 1$.\n\nHowever, this only works if $m$ is prime. If $m$ is composite, Carolyn can continue to her second move by forming the set $\\mathcal{F}'.$\n\nWithout loss of generality, let $\\mathcal{F'}$ only contain the pair $(2, 3)$ as the prime factors of $m$. Carolyn must remove $3$ to prevent Paul from removing both $2$ and $3$. This leaves Carolyn $2$ numbers to remove, but not all of her divisors!\n\nWe return to the first move strategy without renaming $m$ as prime so as to take advantage of the structure. Carolyn removes $2$ and then blocks Paul's number $2$ in the next round with her $2$. Carolyn can then choose any $3$ to remove, with the remaining two being kept for the last round. By this strategy, Carolyn's number is $2 + 3 + (2 + (k_2 - 1)) = 7 + k_2 - 1,$ where $k_2$ is the smallest prime number greater than $3$.\n\nTo find Paul's sum, we can add up the sums Carolyn chooses, which are $7 + k_2 - 1$, $2$, and $k_2 + (1 + (k_2 - 1))$, giving $7 + k_2 - 1 + 2 + k_2 + 1 + k_2 - 1 = 11 + 3k_2$. When $k_2 = 5$, $11 + 3k_2 = 20$. Notice that in all cases $2$, $4$, and $6$ are kept as the final numbers being removed. We observe that Carolyn has removed $2$ is no more than $3$ turns depending on the initial factors $a_1, a_2, ..., a_p$, with $p$ being the count of prime factors $a_i$ equaling $2$. $2$ is removed a maximum of $3$ times (if no composite factors into $m$), while for the rest of the moves, $m$ is removed consecutively for two turns and $3$ is removed for one turn. By induction, Carolyn would pick a number at least $m + p - 1$ turns depending on the count of prime factors squared.\n\nTherefore, Carolyn was able to remove at most $m + p + 3$ numbers, resulting in a sum of\n\\begin{align*}\nm + p + (m + (p + 1)) + (m + p + 1) + (m + p + 3) & = 7m + 4p + 6 = \\boxed{20}.\n\\end{align*} \\[\n\\blacksquare\n\\ $\\blacksquare$",
