      "text": "To determine the sum of the numbers that Carolyn removes if \\( n = 6 \\) and she removes the integer \\( 2 \\) on her first turn, we need to carefully analyze the game rules and the sequence of moves.\n\n1. **Initial Setup**: The game starts with the numbers \\( \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\} \\).\n\n2. **Carolyn's First Move**: Carolyn removes the number \\( 2 \\). The list of remaining numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\{1, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Paul's First Move**: Paul removes all the positive divisors of \\( 2 \\), which are \\( 1 \\). The list of remaining numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\{1, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Carolyn's Second Move**: Carolyn now needs to remove a number from the list \\( \\{1, 3, 4, 5, 6\\} \\). The only number she can remove is \\( 1 \\). After removing \\( 1 \\), the list is:\n   \\[\n   \\{3, 4, 5, 6\\}\n   \\]\n   Consequently, Paul does not remove any numbers since \\( 1 \\) is removed.\n\n5. **Paul's Second Move**: Continue this process until Carolyn cannot remove any more numbers. Notice that when Carolyn removes a number, the divisors of that number are removed immediately. The game will end when all numbers are removed or an error is encountered.\n\n6. **Game Analysis**: By following the rules, we can identify how the game progresses. The sequence will end when all prime numbers are removed and all composite numbers are paired according to their divisors. Here's a summary of the removals:\n   - Carolyn removes \\( 2 \\).\n   - Paul removes \\( 1 \\).\n   - Carolyn removes \\( 1 \\) (from \\( 3 \\)).\n   - Paul removes \\( 1 \\) (from \\( 4 \\)).\n   - Carolyn removes \\( 2 \\) (from \\( 4 \\)).\n   - Paul removes \\( 1 \\) (from \\( 5 \\)).\n   - Carolyn removes \\( 5 \\).\n   - Paul removes \\( 1 \\) (from \\( 5 \\)).\n   - Carolyn removes \\( 4 \\).\n   - Paul removes \\( 1 \\) (from \\( 6 \\)).\n   - Carolyn removes \\( 6 \\).\n   - Paul removes \\( 1 \\) (from \\( 6 \\)).\n\n   This process continues until all numbers are removed. Since the game structure is symmetrical, we can conclude that Carolyn will always pair with an odd number removal to remove the next prime number or to remove the last composite number.\n\n7. **Sum Calculation**: Summing up the numbers Carolyn removes \\( 2, 2, 2, 2, 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 10\n   \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the numbers that Carolyn removes is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
